#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2b25a9c-a983-580c-878e-4224b7548f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294498} {:answer/id #uuid "5a40dba0-67d5-58e4-8b61-71b97c14184d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294499} {:answer/id #uuid "d4384449-e97e-50b5-b015-95f2f8b5633b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294500} {:answer/id #uuid "9b46aa2b-5494-54c2-99b8-f73a9e9492ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294501} {:answer/id #uuid "232133c4-05bd-5487-b748-93227ab76cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a40dba0-67d5-58e4-8b61-71b97c14184d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294499} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e606b0d-c1e8-5e4e-ac94-039518580d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d3e1743-cd34-559d-bad0-d34093686fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-118786" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\n- $8{y}^{′}-xy=3$\n- $3{y}^{″}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
